# Mobile — account data ends with the session

A phone is signed into one account at a time, and the next account on the same phone must never
read, show, or request the last one's data. When it does, the server answers `403 Forbidden` on an
id the new account does not own, and the new user sees the old user's queue or playlists — a
privacy bug, not only an error.

The server is right to refuse. Never "fix" these by loosening an API ownership check or by
silencing the client's request-error log.

## Classify every store before you write to it

Anything that survives a render — SQLite tables, `kv_meta` keys, AsyncStorage prefs, module-level
variables, React context state, refs, the native CarPlay / Android Auto cache — is one of:

| Kind | Examples | On session end |
| ---- | ---- | ---- |
| **Device data** | subscriptions, add-by-RSS feeds, downloads, `channel_seen`, public directory caches, the last-playback snapshot | **Kept.** Documented in `clearSession` |
| **Account data** | account snapshot, `queue_cache`, playlists and their owned / followed flags, reconcile conflicts, anything fetched from a `*Private` / `/account/*` / `/queue/*` route | **Cleared** |
| **Keyed by account** | `playback_outbox`, `playback_local_state` (`account_id_text` column) | Kept; every read filters on the current account |

A new store is account data unless you can say why a different person on the same phone should
see it. If it is account data and has no `account_id_text` column, it must be cleared.

## Where clearing happens

- **Storage:** `clearSession` in `apps/mobile/src/auth/AuthProvider.tsx`, before it sets
  `status` to `anonymous`. Every way a session ends (`user_logout`, `session_expired`, `reset`)
  goes through it.
- **In-memory stores and module-level caches:** the `status === 'anonymous'` effect in
  `apps/mobile/src/sync/SyncProvider.tsx`, which already drops queued jobs and empties the queue
  store. Add new resets there rather than a second effect in another provider.

A TTL is not a clear. "It expires in five minutes" means five minutes of the wrong account.

## Every account change passes through `anonymous`

No code path may go `authenticated` → `authenticated` for a different account. Sign-in helpers
that can run while already signed in (test shortcuts included) call `clearSession` first. Both
reset points key on the `anonymous` transition, and neither fires on a direct switch.

## Account data written after an `await` must still belong to this session

A running sync job or fetch is not cancelled by sign-out; `syncQueue.reset()` only drops pending
jobs. A write of account data that lands after `clearSession` puts the old account's data back. A
repository that caches account data captures a session generation before the request and skips the
write if the generation changed. Advance the generation where the store is cleared.

Auth token refresh uses the same generation idea: `advanceAuthSessionGeneration` runs at the start
of `clearSession`, and `refreshAccessTokenSingleFlight` skips `setTokens` when the generation
changed while it waited — otherwise a late refresh would sign the user back in after logout.

## A 401 must not churn tokens forever

See [`mobile-auth-refresh-terminates`](/.cursor/rules/mobile-auth-refresh-terminates.mdc). Every
authenticated 401 path ends in valid credentials or `clearSession`. Refreshed credentials that are
still rejected are dead; writing them again and re-firing `accessToken`-keyed effects is how a
request loop starts.

## Identity has one source per moment

The token, the account snapshot, and cached ids briefly disagree during a switch. Do not combine
an account id from one source with a token from another (for example the outbox drain reading
`accountRepository.getSnapshot()` while calling the API with the context's token) unless a session
end always clears both.

